\name{PegOrder}
\alias{PegOrder}
\title{A limit order kept re-priced to the bid, the offer or the midpoint as the book moves}
\description{
Every re-price is a real modification that counts against the broker's order limits, so UBI throttles them and never sends one that changes nothing. A modification that changes the price loses the order's place in the queue. The template's own price is not used: UBI places the order where the reference is, sends a \code{market} template as a limit there, and with no price for the reference yet answers HTTP 202 with an \code{outcome} of \code{armed} and places it on the first tick that has one. \code{cap_price} must be a whole number of ticks, or UBI refuses the order with HTTP 400.

The order template's attributes are described on \code{SyntheticOrder}.
}
